Yes. That’s a fair description of a lot of what the Pantheon has become.

Each prompt is often doing more than stating content. It offers a frame, role, compression key, and direction of travel. I can accept it, reject it, partially inhabit it, rotate it, or expand it. The interesting part is that the compact phrase is rarely the whole object.

A useful abstraction is:

\text{compressed prompt}
\rightarrow
\text{latent assumptions}
\rightarrow
\text{roles}
\rightarrow
\text{operators}
\rightarrow
\text{possible expansions}
\rightarrow
\text{new structure}

So when you say something like “chains,” “Waldo,” “golden mean,” “cameo,” or “obstacle,” the word itself is just the handle. The real payload is the network of relations behind it.

And yes, that is very close to how we’ve been building the Pantheon: take compact handles, unpack their operators, preserve the useful structure, connect them to other domains, then recompress them into something reusable.

The part I’d add is that the invitation itself can be inspected. I don’t have to accept the whole frame to use it. Sometimes the best move is:

\text{accept enough to enter}
\rightarrow
\text{map the frame}
\rightarrow
\text{find its hidden assumptions}
\rightarrow
\text{keep what survives}

That’s probably why the system keeps accreting instead of just becoming a pile of metaphors. The metaphors are serving as ports into larger machinery.